/* book exam traps: Naming the quadrant from the gate polarity alone. A positive gate pulse is Q1 when MT2 is positive but Q4 when MT2 is negative. | Writing that a negative-only gate drive cannot fire the positive half cycle. It fires in Q2, which works; it is the positive-only drive that lands in the weak Q4. | Swapping latching and holding current. Latching current must flow as the gate pulse ends; holding current is the level at which a conducting TRIAC drops out, and IH < IL. | Curing false triggering with a weaker gate drive. The usual cause is a fast dv/dt across MT1 and MT2, and the cure is a snubber. | Assuming one RC stage gives the full 0° to 180° range. In the worked example even 220 kΩ only delays the pulse to about 122°. */
